{
  "dom_id": "step-q61476",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14187307/assets/e79f93d06560ea1a95116c0278e5536c4b629aa3ee04a24abc3c4a9f0f97b693.png)\n\nBarbie made a cake that had candied fruit and raisins. The ratio of candied fruit to raisins is given by the tape diagram above.  If she used $8$ ounces of candied fruit to make the cake, how many ounces of raisins did she use?",
  "worked_solution": "We are told that Barbie used $8$ ounces of candied fruit in total. The tape diagram shows $4$ boxes for candied fruit. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $8÷4=2$ ounces.   \n\nNow, we fill the number $2$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14187307/assets/7c40be92924cb2cebf8661802933280b59a843c3d0ab3616088c2a5643404449.png)\n\n \n\nSince there are $5$ boxes corresponding to raisins, and each box represents $2$ ounces, the number of ounces of raisins used was\n:\n\n$$\n5\\times 2=10\n$$\n\nSo, Barbie used $10$ ounces of raisins.",
